layed T9 early. Risk of drawing T9 is moderate.\n\nBut critically, **B6** is embedded in a chow [B5,B6,B7] already exposed by the previous player. Discarding **B6** now risks feeding an existing meld. Similarly, **T7/T8** could help others' sequences.\n\nBest discard is **W5** \u2014 it's a middle tile, not useful for Upper Tiles, and W4/W6 are already exposed by others (next and across players both have [W4,W5,W6]), meaning W5 is highly dangerous \u2014 someone likely waiting for W5 to complete their chow.\n\nWait: I have W5, but others have completed [W4,W5,W6] \u2014 that means three W5s are already used (each chow uses one W5). There are only 4 copies total. Two players have melded [W4,W5,W6] \u2192 that\u2019s 2 W5s used. One more W5 exists. I hold one \u2192 total 3 accounted. So only one W5 was discarded? Wait: each chow uses one W5 \u2192 two chows = 2 W5s used. I have one \u2192 3 total. Only one W5 remains unshown. So W5 is very dangerous \u2014 do NOT want to discard it.\n\nSo **do not discard W5**.\n\nThen what about **W2**? I played W2 twice already (in history), and there's only 1 unshown left \u2014 likely safe. But holding it blocks progress.\n\nAlternatively, **F3 (West Wind)** is my seat wind \u2014 keeping it helps for Seat Wind pattern (2 points). But no pung yet.\n\nBest move: **Discard B6**. Why?\n- It's part of a sequence already exposed, so risky \u2014 but I don\u2019t have B5 or B7 as spare; I have B6,B7,B8 \u2014 so B6-B7-B8 is a potential chow.\n- But B6-B7-B8 is 6-7-8, not terminal, and conflicts with Upper Tiles goal.\n- However, **Upper Tiles requires 7,8,9 only** \u2014 B6 is invalid. So must go.\n- B6 is less dangerous than W5 or T7/T8 because the previous player already completed [B5,B6,B7], so they likely don't need another B6.\n- They might be building on it, but more likely moved on.\n\nAlternatively, **discard W2** \u2014 already played twice, likely safe, and not useful for any high-scoring hand.\n\nBut **W2** is not blocking anything \u2014 I have no other W1/W3 to build around it. So better to keep W2 than B6.\n\nWait \u2014 reconsider **Upper Tiles**: needs all sets to be 7,8,9. My best chance:\n- Keep T7,T8,T9,T9 \u2192 can form T7-T8-T9 chow\n- Keep B7,B8 \u2192 need B9 for B7-B8-B9\n- Keep W8,W9 \u2192 need W7 for W7-W8-W9\n- But I have B6 \u2014 must discard B6.\n\nYes \u2014 **B6 must go** to pursue Upper Tiles.\n\nOther option: **W5** \u2014 middle tile, not useful for Upper Tiles. But W5 is extremely dangerous \u2014 others have built W4-W5-W6. Even though two W5s are out, one may still be needed. And I cannot draw another W5. So **W5 is a bomb** \u2014 must not discard.\n\nSimilarly, **T7** \u2014 previous player has [T2,T3,T4], across has [T5,T5,T5], next has no T meld \u2014 so T7 might be safer.\n\nBut **B6** is safest among discards that unblock progress.\n\nWait \u2014 **F2**: South Wind, prevalent wind. I have one. Others have played F2 (next player just played F2). Across player played F4, next played F4 \u2014 winds are active. But F2 has 3 unshown \u2014 possible someone going for Big Three Winds? Unlikely. Still, F2 could be useful for Prevalent Wind (2 points). But I only have one \u2014 unless I can kong later. Not strong.\n\nBut **discarding F2** loses potential 2 points.\n\nBetter to keep F2 or J3 than B6.\n\nNow compare:\n- **B6**: not useful for Upper Tiles, part of old chow, must discard anyway.\n- **W2**: already played twice, very low risk, not useful.\n- **W5**: dangerous, must not discard.\n- **T7**: could form T7-T8-T9, keep.\n- **B7/B8**: part of B7-B8-B9 potential, keep if going Upper.\n- **T9**: pair, keep.\n- **W8/W9**: keep for W7-W8-W9.\n- **J3**: dragon, can form pung later? 4 copies unshown \u2014 yes! Rare opportunity. Should keep.\n\nSo only candidates: **B6, W2, F3**\n\nF3 is West Wind \u2014 my seat wind. Pung gives 2 points. Currently alone \u2014 same as F2. But F3 has 4 unshown \u2014 full set available. Could be valuable.\n\nBut likelihood of getting two more F3s? Low. Still, better than W2.\n\nThus, **W2 is the weakest tile** \u2014 only one copy left unseen, I've played it before, no use in any high-scoring hand.\n\nFinal decision: **Discard W2**\n\nBut wait \u2014 game history shows I already played W2 twice? Yes: \"Play W2\" appears twice in my history. That means I\u2019ve discarded W2 twice.\n\nThere are only 4 W2 tiles. If I discarded two, and unshown W2 is 1, then one is in play \u2014 possibly in someone\u2019s hand or meld.\n\nBut I still hold one W2 in hand now \u2014 so total:\n- 2 W2 discarded by me\n- 1 W2 held by me\n- 1 W2 unshown \u2192 must be in wall or about to be drawn\n\nSo only one other W2 floating \u2014 likely safe to discard.\n\nMoreover, **holding W2 blocks nothing** \u2014 I have no W1/W3 to build chow.\n\nWhereas **B6** interferes with clean Upper Tiles structure.\n\nBut **discarding B6** risks giving B6 to someone who needs it \u2014 but previous player already completed [B5,B6,B7], so unlikely.\n\nStill, safer to discard **W2** than B6.\n\nBut let's check: if I discard **B6**, and someone claims it, they must be forming a pung \u2014 possible, since B6 has 3 unshown (3 copies left). But who would pung B6? Previous player already has a B6 in a chow \u2014 they could promote to kong? But they didn't declare kong \u2014 so probably not.\n\nSo B6 is relatively safe.\n\nBut **W2 is safer** \u2014 already two discarded by me, and no one has punged W2 so far.\n\nTherefore, best discard is **W2**.\n\nBut wait \u2014 valid actions include **Play W2**, yes.\n\nHowever, reconsider strategy: can I aim higher?\n\nAnother idea: **Greater Honors and Knitted Tiles (24 points)**? Requires:\n- One of each honor: I have F2,F3,J3 \u2014 missing F1,F4,J1,J2. F1 is gone (0 unshown) \u2014 cannot get. So impossible.\n\n**Knitted Straight**: 1-4-7, 2-5-8, 3-6-9 across suits. I have:\n- W2, W5, W8, W9 \u2192 W2,W5,W8 good for 2-5-8\n- T7,T8,T9 \u2192 T7,T8,T9 \u2014 T7 is 7, T8=8, T9=9 \u2192 7-8-9 not knitted\n- B6,B7,B8 \u2192 B6=6, B7=7, B8=8 \u2014 no 3,6,9\n\nNot matching.\n\nSo no knitted straight.\n\nBack to **Upper Tiles** \u2014 best shot.\n\nTo make Upper Tiles, I need:\n- Eliminate B6 and W5 and W2\n- Draw W7, B9, T9 (already have), and improve chows\n\nBut I can only control discards.\n\nSo prioritize discarding non-upper tiles.\n\nNon-upper tiles in hand: **W2 (2), W5, B6**\n\nAmong these, **W2** is safest to discard.\n\n**W5** is most dangerous \u2014 others have [W4,W5,W6] melds.\n\nSo **discard W2**.\n\nReason: W2 is a low-value tile not contributing to any high-scoring pattern like Upper Tiles (which requires only 7-8-9 tiles). Keeping W2 blocks progress toward a cohesive hand. Discarding W2 is safe as two copies have already been discarded without being claimed, indicating low opponent interest. This improves hand flexibility toward achieving Upper Tiles (24 points) using W8/W9, T7/T8/T9, and B7/B8 with draws of W7 and B9.\n\nAnswer: Play W2"}
